          "question": "What sort of dietary change does guidance describe for high blood pressure?",
          "answer": "Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n\nThe guidance I could retrieve only partly answers this. The main source is **\"Diet for high blood pressure\"** (educational dietary guidance). It says:\n\n- **Weight reduction** lowers blood pressure, roughly in proportion to the amount lost. The effect starts early, and a modest, sustained loss is described as worthwhile.\n- **Reducing alcohol** is called one of the more reliable non-drug measures. The excerpt cuts off mid-sentence, so I can't give the details.\n- **Single-ingredient remedies** such as \"superfoods,\" vinegar, and garlic supplements do not have evidence comparable to an overall eating pattern.\n- **Caffeine** raises blood pressure briefly, but regular intake has little lasting effect for most people.\n\nThe retrieved passages do not describe the full dietary pattern, salt (sodium) guidance, or specific foods to eat or avoid for blood pressure. A heart failure document refers to a blood-pressure diet note on sodium, but the excerpt I received doesn't include that content.\n\nTo discuss a personal diet plan, a clinician or registered dietitian is the right person to ask, especially if you take blood pressure medicine.",
